<Project Sdk="Aspire.AppHost.Sdk/13.5.3">

  <!-- Item 4, step 2: somewhere for the spans to go.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

       ── What this project is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
       A local development launcher, and nothing else. It starts
       CreServicing.Api, opens the Aspire dashboard, and injects the OTLP
       endpoint that Api/Telemetry.cs is already watching for. It is not in the
       request path, it is not a deployment artifact, and nothing references it.
       Deleting it would cost the dashboard and not one line of behaviour.

       ── Why an AppHost rather than the standalone dashboard container ──────
       The usual advice is to run mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspire-dashboard in
       Docker and point OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT at it, which is the lighter
       option and needs no project at all. There is no Docker on this machine.

       The AppHost route needs none: Aspire 13 ships as an MSBuild SDK resolved
       from NuGet — note the Sdk attribute above rather than a PackageReference
       — so the workload install that made this expensive under Aspire 8 is
       gone. Same dashboard, no daemon.

       ── Why 13.5.3 and not section 5's 13.2.3 ─────────────────────────────
       This started pinned to 13.2.3 to match the instructor's
       MinimalAgent/AppHost — one Aspire version in the repo, one thing to keep
       straight. That pin restored a transitive MessagePack 2.5.192, which
       carries eleven advisories including two rated high (GHSA-hv8m-jj95-wg3x,
       GHSA-vh6j-jc39-fggf): 22 NU1902/NU1903 warnings on every build.

       13.5.3 resolves a MessagePack without them, and the count goes to zero.
       Matching the scratchpad was never worth two known-high advisories, and
       raising the SDK is the fix rather than the workaround — pinning
       MessagePack directly would have meant forcing a major version on
       Aspire's own IPC serializer to paper over a dependency it controls.

       Section 5's AppHost stays on 13.2.3. It is a follow-along scratchpad that
       nothing builds; this is the project that gets shown.

       ── Why CI does not build this ────────────────────────────────────────
       CI builds through the two test projects, and no test references this one,
       so it is excluded by construction rather than by an exclusion rule. That
       is the correct outcome: an AppHost with no tests would add an Aspire SDK
       restore to every CI run in order to compile a launcher. See the note in
       .github/workflows/ci.yml, which anticipated this project existing. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>CreServicing.AppHost</RootNamespace>

    <!-- ASPIRE010: 13.5 wants AspireUseCliBundle=true so the `aspire` CLI's
         run and publish features work. This project is launched with
         `dotnet run` and exists to open a dashboard, so the bundle would be a
         download in exchange for features nothing here calls.

         Suppressed rather than enabled, and named explicitly rather than
         blanket-silenced. Worth being clear about the difference from the
         advisories above: that was a security warning and it got fixed by
         moving the dependency, not muted. This one is a capability notice. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);ASPIRE010</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\CreServicing.Api\CreServicing.Api.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
